LD1 with 3 and 4 destination registers -- restore the semantics.

`LD1_ASISDLSE_R3_3V`, `R4_4V`, `LD1_ASISDLSEP_I3_I3` and `I4_I4` had no live
`DEF_ISEL`. Their decoders exist and are correct, so each one DECODED and then
lifted to nothing: a silent no-op that loaded not one of its three or four
destination vectors. Measured 2026-09-01 -- every vector read back as zero, with
no diagnostic of any kind (`RAPTORMARK_ECV_UNDEC_CENSUS=1` reports nothing,
because the decoder never refused).

❗ NO FRAMEWORK CHANGE WAS NEEDED, contrary to the first estimate. The lifter
already handles an N-valued return generically -- `VroInstructionLifter` does

    if (auto struct_ty = dyn_cast<StructType>(sema_inst->getType()))
      CHECK(struct_ty->getNumElements() == write_regs.size());

and then indexes the result once per destination register. `TPair` was not
special-cased anywhere; it is just a two-field packed struct. So `TTriple` and
`TQuad` beside it are the whole of the missing piece.

Defined ONCE, outside the `#if defined(__x86_64__)` split that the pair forms
live in. The x86 pair path returns an `_ecv_u<W>v2_t` VECTOR via
reinterpret_cast, and there is no v3/v4 type to mirror that with; a plain struct
is what the lifter wants in both arms anyway.

The POST-INDEX variants delegate to the non-post-index ones. The base-register
update is not the semantics' job -- `AddPostIndexMemOp` puts it on the memory
operand and `VroInstructionLifter` applies it after the call, which its own
comment gives the reason for ("If we update in the semantics function, we should
increase the num of return variables and that will occur the returning through
memory"). Performing it here would update the base register twice.

Companion to 0072, which did the same for the 2-register post-index form. With
both applied, every LD1 multiple-structures form is implemented. Still missing
after this: five ST1 forms that have no `TryDecode*` at all, and the whole
register-post-index (`[Xn], Xm`) family -- both of which fail LOUDLY rather than
silently, so they cannot corrupt a result.

